3. The guard-ORDERING proof (security-spec.md Boundary B3), structural
==============================================================================

--- 3a. The landed function, mcp/sdd-forge-mcp/src/path-guard.ts:293-332 ---

  293  export function listGuardedFilesWithDiagnostics(
  294    root: SddRoot,
  295    relDir: string,
  296  ): GuardedListResult {
  297    const guardResult = resolveGuardedDirectory(root, relDir);
  298    if (!guardResult.ok) {
  299      return { files: [], errors: [{ path: relDir, reason: guardResult.error.message }] };
  300    }
  301
  302    const files: string[] = [];
  303    const errors: GuardedListError[] = [];
  304    const walk = (absDir: string, relPrefix: string): void => {
  305      let entries: string[];
  306      try {
  307        entries = readdirSync(absDir);
  308      } catch (error) {
  309        errors.push({ path: relPrefix, reason: errorMessage(error) });
  310        return;
  311      }
  312      for (const entry of entries) {
  313        const absEntryPath = join(absDir, entry);
  314        const relEntryPath = relPrefix.length > 0 ? `${relPrefix}/${entry}` : entry;
  315        let stats: ReturnType<typeof statSync>;
  316        try {
  317          stats = statSync(absEntryPath);
  318        } catch (error) {
  319          errors.push({ path: relEntryPath, reason: errorMessage(error) });
  320          continue;
  321        }
  322        if (stats.isDirectory()) {
  323          walk(absEntryPath, relEntryPath);
  324        } else if (stats.isFile()) {
  325          files.push(relEntryPath);
  326        }
  327      }
  328    };
  329
  330    walk(guardResult.data.resolvedPath, relDir.replace(/\/+$/, ""));
  331    return { files, errors };
  332  }

Read off the source directly:

  * line 297 -- resolveGuardedDirectory is the FIRST statement of the function.
    It is at function-body scope, not inside any `try`.
  * lines 298-300 -- its failure returns IMMEDIATELY. `errors` (line 303) and
    `walk` (line 304) do not even exist yet at that point, so no `readdirSync`
    or `statSync` can have been reached on a denied path. The denial's `errors`
    entry is built inline from `guardResult.error.message`, i.e. from the
    guard's own hard-deny text, and is structurally distinct from the
    error-COLLECTION path that lines 309/319 use.
  * the first `try` in the whole function is at line 306, three lines after the
    guard has already succeeded, and it is inside `walk`, which is only ever
    invoked at line 330 with `guardResult.data.resolvedPath` -- a path the
    guard has already approved. There is no code path on which `walk` runs
    against an unapproved path.
  * the two `catch` blocks (308, 318) push to `errors` and then `return` /
    `continue` exactly as the pre-change bare `catch {}` blocks did. Validation
    and error-collection are therefore in disjoint regions of the function.

--- 3b. The walk body vs. the pre-change listGuardedFiles body, diffed ---

`diff -u` between the region `const results: string[] = [] ... return results;`
of HEAD~1's listGuardedFiles and the region
`const files: string[] = [] ... return { files, errors };` of the new function:

--- walk.before.txt
+++ walk.after.txt
@@ -1,9 +1,11 @@
-  const results: string[] = [];
+  const files: string[] = [];
+  const errors: GuardedListError[] = [];
   const walk = (absDir: string, relPrefix: string): void => {
     let entries: string[];
     try {
       entries = readdirSync(absDir);
-    } catch {
+    } catch (error) {
+      errors.push({ path: relPrefix, reason: errorMessage(error) });
       return;
     }
     for (const entry of entries) {
@@ -12,17 +14,18 @@
       let stats: ReturnType<typeof statSync>;
       try {
         stats = statSync(absEntryPath);
-      } catch {
+      } catch (error) {
+        errors.push({ path: relEntryPath, reason: errorMessage(error) });
         continue;
       }
       if (stats.isDirectory()) {
         walk(absEntryPath, relEntryPath);
       } else if (stats.isFile()) {
-        results.push(relEntryPath);
+        files.push(relEntryPath);
       }
     }
   };

   walk(guardResult.data.resolvedPath, relDir.replace(/\/+$/, ""));
-  return results;
+  return { files, errors };
 }

Every changed line is one of exactly four things: the accumulator rename
(results -> files), the added `errors` accumulator, the two `catch {}` ->
`catch (error) { errors.push(...) }` conversions, and the return shape. The
walk's CONTROL FLOW is untouched: still `return` past a top-level readdirSync
failure, still `continue` past a per-entry statSync failure, same recursion,
same isDirectory/isFile branch. REQ-003 adds visibility, not a stricter walk.


