4. MUTATION EXPERIMENT -- the ordering assertions are NOT vacuous
==============================================================================

A structural argument that the guard runs first is worth little if the tests
would pass anyway. So the naive refactor security-spec.md B3 names was actually
APPLIED to the landed path-guard.ts and this task's own suite was re-run
against it.

The mutant (applied on top of Commit A, then discarded -- see 4c):

  -  const guardResult = resolveGuardedDirectory(root, relDir);
  -  if (!guardResult.ok) {
  -    return { files: [], errors: [{ path: relDir, reason: guardResult.error.message }] };
  -  }
  -
     const files: string[] = [];
     const errors: GuardedListError[] = [];
     const walk = (absDir: string, relPrefix: string): void => { ...unchanged... };

  -  walk(guardResult.data.resolvedPath, relDir.replace(/\/+$/, ""));
  +  let absScanDir: string;
  +  try {
  +    const guardResult = resolveGuardedDirectory(root, relDir);
  +    if (!guardResult.ok) {
  +      errors.push({ path: relDir, reason: guardResult.error.message });
  +    }
  +    absScanDir = guardResult.ok
  +      ? guardResult.data.resolvedPath
  +      : join(root.path, relDir);
  +  } catch (error) {
  +    errors.push({ path: relDir, reason: errorMessage(error) });
  +    return { files, errors };
  +  }
  +  walk(absScanDir, relDir.replace(/\/+$/, ""));
     return { files, errors };

This is deliberately the SUBTLE version of the defect, not a strawman: the
mutant still records the denial in `errors[]`, still uses the guard's own
message, and still puts the right `path` on the entry. A test that merely
asserted `errors.length >= 1` and checked the reason text would pass against
it. What the mutant also does -- silently -- is let the walk proceed against
the denied directory, which is precisely B3's "a denylist rejection ...
silently reinterpreted as a mere I/O error rather than a hard deny".

